+## When to Use
+
+- **Automating native desktop apps** — Finder/Explorer, Mail/Outlook/Thunderbird, Figma, Logic, native chat clients, IDEs, terminal emulators
+- **Interacting with web UIs that the browser tool can't handle** — CAPTCHAs, Electron consent dialogs, browser-based SSO flows, WebAuthn prompts
+- **Dragging and dropping files between app windows** — the browser tools have no cross-app drag capability
+- **Extracting information from non-web sources** — desktop-only tools, legacy enterprise apps, native settings panels
+- **Performing multi-app workflows** — copy data from a web app, paste into a native spreadsheet, save and attach to an email
+- **Verifying visual UI state** — capture a screenshot of an app to check that an operation completed (e.g., file saved, dialog dismissed)
+- **Testing desktop app behavior** — click through UI flows in CI or smoke-test environments
+
+## Not For
+
+- **Simple web automation** → use `browser_*` tools (headless Chromium, more reliable)
+- **File editing** → use `read_file` / `write_file` / `patch`, not GUI `type` into an editor
+- **Shell commands** → use `terminal`, not `type` into Terminal.app / Windows Terminal
+- **Password entry or payment UI interaction** → hard rule; never touch these surfaces
+- **Private personal apps without user approval** (banking, email, messaging) → ask first
+- **Permission dialogs, 2FA challenges, or anything asking for secrets** → never
+- **Pure CLI operations** → the terminal tool is faster and more precise
+- **Large-scale repetitive input** → use scripts, not desktop automation
+
